def lesson(id,title,body,activity,source):
 return dict(id=id,title=title,html=body+f'<p class="source-note"><strong>Learning sources:</strong> {source}. Activities and fictional examples are newly written for this course.</p>',activity=activity,minutes=20,sourceLabel='Career and further education extension')
def q(text,opts,answer,why):return dict(q=text,options=opts,answer=answer,why=why)
def mod(id,title,goal,output,ls,qs):return dict(id=id,title=title,goal=goal,output=output,verb='Build your future',lessons=ls,quiz=qs)
C['modules'] += [mod(11,'Build your foundations','Understand yourself and create habits that support your next step.','A strengths profile, weekly study plan and digital skills baseline',[
lesson('foundation-identity','Strengths, interests and work values','''<h3>Start with evidence about yourself</h3><p>An interest is something you enjoy exploring. A skill is something you can do. A value is something that matters to you. Keep these separate when considering a course or career: enjoying science does not tell you whether you prefer laboratory work, teaching or engineering.</p><p>List three experiences from school, home, clubs or community life. For each, describe what you did, what was difficult, what you learned and what someone else noticed. Small, repeated contributions count as evidence.</p><h3>Example: from a task to a strength</h3><p>Fictional learner Eric organised tools for a school project and checked that everyone returned them. This gives him evidence of organisation and reliability. It does not yet prove that he should become an engineer. He can test that interest through a project or a conversation with someone in the field.</p><h3>Rank your priorities</h3><p>Choose five values such as helping others, creativity, stability, independence, teamwork or solving problems. Explain your top two with real examples. Career assessments can suggest possibilities; they should not decide your future or limit what you explore.</p><h3>Check your work</h3><p>A useful profile connects each claimed strength to an experience and identifies one skill you want to develop.</p>''','Write three strengths with evidence, two interests to explore and your top two work values. Ask someone who knows your work for one specific observation.','Empower Your Future, Unit 1: Know Yourself; Unit 2: Positive Identity, Occupational Interests and Work Values'),
lesson('foundation-goals','Turn a future goal into a weekly plan','''<h3>Work backwards from a goal</h3><p>A long-term goal gives direction. A short-term goal tells you what to do next. Write an action, a measure and a review date. Choose actions you can control: research completed, practice attempted or feedback requested. Admission decisions remain outside your control.</p><h3>Example: make the task manageable</h3><p>Instead of “I will get a scholarship,” fictional learner Aline writes: “By Friday, I will compare the eligibility and full cost of three programmes and discuss one funding question with my counsellor.” She schedules two 30-minute research sessions and keeps an offline copy of her notes.</p><h3>Plan a realistic week</h3><p>Put classes, rest and existing responsibilities on the calendar first. Add two focused work sessions and a small buffer for a delayed internet connection or unanswered email. At the end of the week, record what happened and adjust the next step.</p><h3>Study actively</h3><p>After a short reading, close it and explain the main point from memory. Check what you missed, then practise with a question or example. Keep an error log: mistake, explanation, next practice task. Ask for help when a repeated difficulty blocks progress.</p>''','Write one long-term goal and two actions for this week. Give each a time, evidence of completion, likely obstacle and backup plan.','CRC Further Education Pathway, EY academic planning and study skills; Empower Your Future, Short-Term Goal Setting, Long-Term Goal Setting and Time Management'),
lesson('foundation-digital','Digital habits for study and applications','''<h3>Build a clear application workspace</h3><p>Use folders such as Research, Records, Essays and Submitted. Give drafts clear names, for example Personal_Essay_2026-09-30_v2. Keep a separate final copy of what you submit. Use an email address you can keep accessing after graduation and check it regularly.</p><h3>Measure typing progress fairly</h3><p>Use the same short practice passage and duration for your baseline and later checks. Record words per minute and accuracy. Practise briefly and regularly; correct work matters more than speed. The supplied curriculum proposes a 50% improvement goal, which is a school learning target, not an admissions requirement.</p><p>Fictional example: increasing from 20 to 30 words per minute is a 50% increase. Going faster while making many more errors is not the intended improvement.</p><h3>Use AI with integrity</h3><p>Follow your school and application rules. When permitted, you might ask for an explanation of an unfamiliar term or questions to help you reflect. Check factual claims yourself. Keep your experiences and final writing truthful and your own. Do not upload another person’s records, passwords or private financial information.</p><h3>Before sending or posting</h3><p>Check the audience, tone, attachments and personal information. On a shared computer, sign out of accounts and download your course workbook before clearing your saved course data.</p>''','Record a typing baseline and accuracy, design four folder names, and write two rules you will follow for AI use and shared computers. Keep passwords out of your notes.','CRC Further Education Pathway, digital skillbuilding; Empower Your Future, Technology Etiquette in the Workplace'),
lesson('foundation-communication','Introduce yourself and ask for support','''<h3>A useful introduction has a purpose</h3><p>In about 30 seconds, say who you are, name an interest with one example, and explain what you want to learn. Speak naturally rather than memorising a speech word for word.</p><p>Fictional example: “I am an S4 student interested in how technology solves everyday problems. I helped my group organise a club attendance sheet. I would like to understand what you study in an information systems course.”</p><h3>Write a clear request</h3><p>Use a specific subject line, a short introduction and one question. Example: “Subject: Question about programme entry requirements. Dear Admissions Team, I am studying the Rwanda A-Level curriculum and researching your computing programme. Could you direct me to the entry requirements for my qualification? Thank you.” Check the website first and avoid requesting information already clearly explained there.</p><h3>Listen and respond</h3><p>Let the other person finish. Summarise what you understood and clarify any uncertainty. When receiving feedback, ask for an example and choose a change you can test. Respect another person’s time and right to decline.</p>''','Draft a 30-second introduction and one email question. Practise with a partner, then revise one sentence using their feedback.','Empower Your Future, Communication Skills, Interpersonal Skills and Highlighting Your Strengths/Elevator Speech')
],[q('Which statement gives evidence of a strength?',['I am the best leader.','I organised three meetings and followed up on agreed tasks.','A quiz said I must be a manager.'],1,'Describe an action and its context. A label or assessment result alone is not evidence.'),q('Which goal is most actionable this week?',['Be successful.','Receive an admission offer.','Compare three programmes and record one question for each by Friday.'],2,'This goal has a clear action, measure and review point within the learner’s control.'),q('What should you do before using AI for application work?',['Check the rules and keep your experiences and writing authentic.','Paste confidential records into any tool.','Submit generated experiences as your own.'],0,'Rules vary. Keep your work truthful and protect personal information.')]),
mod(12,'Explore education and careers','Investigate different pathways using evidence and real conversations.','A pathway comparison and a supervised experience plan',[
lesson('explore-pathways','Compare university, TVET and other routes','''<h3>Begin with the work you want to understand</h3><p>Further education can include university programmes, technical and vocational education and training (TVET), and other structured learning routes. Compare what a programme teaches and where it can lead. A familiar title or impressive advertisement is not enough.</p><h3>Build a comparison</h3><p>For each option record: qualification awarded, entry requirements, duration, practical learning, recognition, total costs, possible funding and next steps after completion. Record an official source and the date you checked it. Leave unknown facts marked “to verify.”</p><h3>Fictional example</h3><p>Diane enjoys repairing devices. She compares a practical electronics training programme, an engineering degree and a supervised introductory placement. They differ in purpose and entry requirements. She asks what qualification each awards and whether it meets the requirements of her intended career.</p><h3>Keep destinations open</h3><p>Research options in Rwanda and elsewhere in Africa as well as other regions when relevant. The curriculum’s goal of profiles for at least eight institutions across three countries is a research exercise, not a requirement to apply abroad. Build a final list around fit, eligibility and affordability.</p>''','Compare three possible routes using qualification, entry requirements, duration, learning experience and full cost. Identify one unanswered question per route.','CRC Further Education Pathway, introduction to further education and university research; Empower Your Future, Education and Training'),
lesson('explore-research','Research a career and connect it to a course','''<h3>Investigate tasks, not just job titles</h3><p>Find out what a person does in a normal week, which skills they use, how people enter the field and which parts are challenging. Similar job titles can involve different responsibilities. Separate one person’s experience from a general requirement.</p><h3>Use a simple evidence table</h3><p>Record each claim, its source, its date and a question to verify. Use programme pages for course requirements and relevant professional bodies for recognition questions. For local working conditions, compare several current sources and ask someone familiar with the field.</p><h3>Example: follow your curiosity</h3><p>Fictional learner Patrick likes biology and working with data. He investigates laboratory science, public health and teaching. He compares the tasks and training for each before choosing courses to research. An interest in a subject can connect to several careers.</p><h3>Prepare for a college information session</h3><p>Choose two questions that the website has not answered, such as how a practical placement is organised or what support first-year students use. Afterwards, record one useful answer and one follow-up. You can learn through a virtual session when travel is impractical.</p>''','Create two career profiles: typical tasks, skills, training, source and date, personal fit and unresolved question. Link each to a course you want to investigate.','Empower Your Future, Exploring Careers and Career Research; CRC Further Education Pathway, strategic university selection'),
lesson('explore-interview','Learn through an informational interview','''<h3>A learning conversation is different from a job interview</h3><p>An informational interview helps you understand someone’s work. Ask a teacher, counsellor or trusted adult to help identify an appropriate contact. Request a short conversation and explain its learning purpose.</p><h3>Prepare five questions</h3><ul><li>What tasks take up most of your week?</li><li>Which skills do you use most often?</li><li>What surprised you when you began?</li><li>What training helped you, and what should I verify for today?</li><li>What small activity could help me explore this field?</li></ul><h3>Make the conversation respectful</h3><p>Arrive prepared, ask permission before recording and avoid collecting confidential workplace information. A conversation does not guarantee an internship or recommendation. Afterwards, write a brief thank-you identifying one thing you learned.</p><h3>Reflect critically</h3><p>Record what matched your expectations and what changed them. Check whether the person’s pathway is still possible and relevant to you. One person’s salary or experience should not be treated as a promise about your future.</p>''','Draft an invitation, five questions and a thank-you message. After a real or classroom practice conversation, record two insights and one fact to verify.','Empower Your Future, Informational Interviews/Job Shadows and Cover and Thank You Letters'),
lesson('explore-experience','Plan a useful holiday project or internship','''<h3>Choose a learning goal first</h3><p>A good experience helps you practise a skill, understand a field or contribute to a real need. A local project can be useful when travel or a formal internship is not possible. Choose a manageable task and agree how a responsible adult will support you.</p><h3>Example: a small community project</h3><p>Fictional learner Aline plans to help organise reading materials during the holiday. Her goal is to practise record keeping and communication. She agrees her tasks with the supervisor, checks the schedule and transport, and plans to produce a simple inventory without recording private details about readers.</p><h3>Check the arrangement</h3><p>Before starting, clarify the supervisor, tasks, hours, location, costs, permissions and how to ask for help. Follow school procedures for placements. If a task seems unsafe or outside what you agreed, pause and speak to the supervisor or school contact.</p><h3>Keep a learning journal</h3><p>After each session, note what you did, a skill you used, a difficulty and your next step. Ask for specific feedback. At the end, write an honest activities entry describing your own contribution without inflating hours or outcomes.</p>''','Write a one-page experience plan: goal, task, supervisor, schedule, costs, permissions, support contact, evidence and reflection date.','CRC Further Education Pathway, S4/S5 vacation planning; Empower Your Future, Internships, Motivation and Initiative, and Workplace Safety')
],[q('What makes a pathway comparison useful?',['Choose the most famous name.','Compare entry requirements, learning, recognition and full costs.','Assume every qualification leads to the same work.'],1,'Compare the details of each route and verify missing information.'),q('What is the main purpose of an informational interview?',['Guarantee a job.','Collect private company information.','Learn about someone’s work and ask informed questions.'],2,'It is a learning conversation, not a promise of employment.'),q('Before starting an internship, what should you clarify?',['Tasks, supervision, schedule, costs and support arrangements.','Only whether it looks impressive.','Only the organisation’s logo.'],0,'A useful experience needs clear expectations and appropriate support.')]),
mod(13,'Prepare for life beyond school','Apply your strengths in applications, teamwork and a realistic transition plan.','A career portfolio and 90-day transition plan',[
lesson('career-portfolio','Build a portfolio that shows your skills','''<h3>Collect evidence, then select it</h3><p>A portfolio is a small, organised collection of work that shows what you can do. Include a strengths profile, programme research, a CV, selected projects and short reflections. Keep records of responsibilities and dates accurate. Do not include private information about other people.</p><h3>Make transferable skills visible</h3><p>A transferable skill works in more than one setting. Organising a club event can demonstrate planning; explaining a difficult exercise can demonstrate communication. Describe the action and evidence rather than simply listing “teamwork” or “leadership.”</p><p>Fictional example: “Worked with two classmates to organise 60 donated books by topic and created a catalogue for the club.” This shows an identifiable contribution. Use numbers only when you can support them.</p><h3>Tailor the CV or motivation letter</h3><p>Read the opportunity’s instructions. Select relevant experience and explain why the learning or role fits your goals. A motivation letter connects your interest, preparation and intended contribution to that specific opportunity. It is not a copy of every item on your CV.</p><h3>Ask references properly</h3><p>Choose people who know your work. Ask permission, share the opportunity and deadline, and offer a factual summary of your experiences. Never invent or submit a recommendation on someone else’s behalf.</p>''','List five portfolio items and what each proves. Draft two evidence-based CV bullets and a short motivation paragraph for a real opportunity you are researching.','Empower Your Future, Transferable Skills, Resume, Cover Letters and Building a Network/References; CRC Further Education Pathway, motivational letters and application documentation'),
lesson('career-interview','Practise interviews and professional teamwork','''<h3>Prepare examples rather than scripts</h3><p>For a question about a challenge, explain the situation, your task, the action you took and the result or lesson. Describe your own role honestly and give credit to collaborators. It is acceptable to pause or ask the interviewer to repeat a question.</p><p>Fictional example: a group missed a project deadline because responsibilities were unclear. One learner created a task list, confirmed owners and arranged a check-in. The reflection explains what changed and what they would do earlier next time.</p><h3>Respond to disagreement constructively</h3><p>Describe the issue without attacking the person. Listen to their view, identify the shared task and agree one next action. For example: “We have two versions of the report. Can we agree which file to edit and check it together tomorrow?”</p><h3>Use feedback</h3><p>Ask for an example, summarise the suggested change and decide what to practise. In a mock interview, a partner can note whether your answer was specific, clear and relevant. After a real conversation, thank the person without assuming a favourable decision.</p>''','Draft one interview example using situation, task, action and result. Practise it with a partner and record one improvement. Write a respectful response to a group disagreement.','Empower Your Future, Job Interviews, Positive Working Relationships, Interpersonal Skills and Job Retention'),
lesson('career-budget','Build a simple transition budget','''<h3>Separate money confirmed from money hoped for</h3><p>List confirmed resources separately from pending scholarships or possible earnings. Then list regular costs, one-time setup costs and a small contingency where possible. Use a consistent currency and label estimates. A budget makes a gap visible; it does not make funding appear.</p><h3>Fictional monthly exercise in RWF</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr><tr><td>Confirmed resources</td><td>80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Transport</td><td>20,000</td></tr><tr><td>Meals</td><td>35,000</td></tr><tr><td>Data and supplies</td><td>15,000</td></tr><tr><td>Contingency</td><td>10,000</td></tr></table></div><p>The total planned cost is 80,000 RWF. There is no unallocated amount. If transport rises by 5,000 RWF, the learner must revise the plan or find confirmed support. These invented amounts are for arithmetic practice, not local price estimates, and do not include tuition or housing.</p><h3>Ask clear questions</h3><p>When comparing offers, check what is included, when payments are due and what happens in later years. For employment, ask for an explanation of pay, deductions and payment dates using the relevant local rules. The supplied career pack’s US payroll forms and banking examples should not be used as Rwanda instructions.</p>''','Create a fictional or non-sensitive monthly budget. Mark confirmed and unconfirmed resources, calculate the balance, and test what happens if one cost increases by 10%.','Empower Your Future, Personal Finance and Budgeting and Payroll Process; course funding lessons'),
lesson('career-transition','Create your next 90 days and ask for help','''<h3>Plan the first step after the decision</h3><p>Whether your next step is university, TVET, work, a project or further preparation, identify the first tasks and the people who can help. A changed plan is an opportunity to review evidence, not a judgement of your worth.</p><h3>Use three checkpoints</h3><p><strong>Days 1–30:</strong> confirm arrangements, gather required documents, identify support contacts and establish a routine. <strong>Days 31–60:</strong> practise one priority skill, request feedback and check your budget. <strong>Days 61–90:</strong> review progress, revise a goal and plan the next learning opportunity.</p><h3>Build a support map</h3><p>List a trusted adult, an academic or training contact, and a practical support contact. For each, note what you could ask and how to reach them. Verify local services with your school or the organisation; the Massachusetts services in the original career pack are examples from another context.</p><h3>When something becomes difficult</h3><p>Identify the immediate problem, choose one manageable action and ask for help early. You can keep sensitive personal reflections offline. Record achievements as well as difficulties so that your next plan reflects what you have learned.</p><h3>Your final portfolio review</h3><p>Explain your preferred pathway, supporting evidence, costs to verify, next action and backup option to a counsellor. Course quizzes are practice checks; a real application still needs its own document and deadline audit.</p>''','Write a 30/60/90-day plan with one action, evidence and support person per stage. Add a backup option and a date to review your plan with a counsellor.','Empower Your Future, Success at Work, Coping, Community Resources, Moving Forward and Long-Term Goal Setting; CRC Further Education Pathway, post-graduation plan')
],[q('Which CV statement is most useful?',['I am perfect at everything.','I helped organise 60 books and created a catalogue with two classmates.','My friend’s achievements are also mine.'],1,'Specific, truthful actions show a skill and recognise the learner’s contribution.'),q('A budget has 80,000 RWF in confirmed resources and 85,000 RWF in costs. What does it show?',['A 5,000 RWF gap to address.','A guaranteed future scholarship.','A 5,000 RWF surplus.'],0,'Costs exceed confirmed resources by 5,000 RWF.'),q('What makes a transition plan useful?',['Only a distant dream.','No changes after it is written.','Actions, checkpoints, support contacts and a backup option.'],2,'Review points and support make it easier to adapt the plan.')])]
